  SHEA TO BURN QURAN, ENCOURAGE OTHERS, IF PAKISTAN EXECUTES BOY, 14

	Hollywood activist Joe Shea said today that he will burn his 
personal copy of the Quran (Koran), the holy writings of Islam, and urge 
Americans across the country to do the same  if Pakistan goes forward 
with the execution of an illiterate 14-year-old Christian boy, Rehmat 
Masih, accused of chalking unknown graffiti on a mosque in a rural 
village in Pakistan.
	 Shea, a former Village Voice correspondent in Pakistan who met 
briefly with the current Prime Minister in 1971, said he is shocked that 
Benazir Bhutto, a Harvard graduate, has not intervened as yet to stop the 
execution, which threatens the success of a coming American tour during 
which she had been expected to take a strong stand against human rights 
violations in India.
	"If we all have the courage to stand up to this ugly outrage, and 
give Islam the choice of a dead boy or a burned holy book, I believe they 
will feel that the life of the boy is not worth the mass destruction of 
the Quran it will precipitate," Shea said.  "While I do not share this 
topsy-turvy belief, I know that the prospect of tens or hundreds of 
thousands of Americans burning copies of the Quran as a result of the 
remarkable cowardice of the Pakistani government will at least give them 
pause, so that they can ask themselves if there is not a better way."
	The 48-year-old editor said he will post his appeal to other 
Americans and human rights activists on the world-wide Internet, and says 
he feels assured that the Pakistani government will intervene.  "If they 
do not, the Prophet's words will be properly valued."
	Shea was the authr of an article in the Feb. 8, 195 Los Angeles
Times opnion pages that strongly criticized the shooting of a graffiti
tagger in Sun Valley on Jan. 30.  "If Dostoyevsky is correct in saying,
'Every man's death diminishes us', then how much more are we diminished as
human beings if we permit this small boy and his father to die when they
were sentenced to die despite any existing evidence of the crime or any
witness with knowledge of the words that were allegedly written."
	Shea, a Catholic, said that he will not be swayed by any threats
he may receive.
