Here's an update on the "tasteless jokes" controversy at the University
of Guelph.  Another article appeared in The Guelph Tribune today. (see below)

Since early January, we've been working with University of Guelph student
Carol Walberg to help her respond to accusations of "human rights abuses"
for her role in running a "tasteless jokes conference" on a university
computer.  Although there were almost 300 willing participants, Carol Walberg
has apparently been singled out.

Through conversations with colleagues on the faculty there, we've
managed to learn more about the policies and procedures under which
the complaint against Carol Walberg is being handled.  We've also
managed to contact Prof. Neal Johnson who agreed to act as her advisor,
and accompany her to all meetings.  One fact that we've uncovered
is that since she wasn't actually a registered student last term,
she can't be disciplined as a student.

The complaint against Carol Walberg still stands, and the human rights
officer is apparently trying to decide the next step, given procedural
constraints.

It is ironic that within 24 hours of Carol Walberg shutting down the
"TCosy jokes conference" last term, another student, Bill Yeo,
recreated the discussion group.  No complaints have been filed against him.

Frankly, we don't understand why Carol Walberg has been singled out
for this kind of treatment.

--
